#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# thread

#% text
# `thread(f(a, b, ...))` gives `[f(a1, b1, ...), f(a2, b2, ...), ...]`, taking the arguments that are lists element by element and repeating the others.
# `thread(x, g)` threads over the arguments with head `g` instead of lists.
# `thread(x, g, spec)` threads only over the arguments `spec` names: `all`, `none`, `n` (the first n), `-n` (the last n), `[i]`, `[i, j]` or `[i, j, step]`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
thread(f([1, 2, 3], [a, b, c]))
#% output
#= [f(1, a), f(2, b), f(3, c)]

#% cell
thread(f([1, 2], y))
#% output
#= [f(1, y), f(2, y)]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Only the last two arguments:

#% cell
thread(f([a, b], [c, d], [u, v]), list, -2)
#% output
#= [f([a, b], c, u), f([a, b], d, v)]

#% text
# Equations from two lists:

#% cell
thread(equal([x, y], [1, 2]))
#% output
#= [x == 1, y == 2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `map_thread`, `map`, `transpose`
